WEBVTT

NOTE
Timings are measured from the synthesised narration by narrate.py. After the
edit, fix the times here and copy them across to 01-captions.ja.vtt — the cue
numbering is the same in both files, so only the timestamps move.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.680
No probe.

2
00:00:01.121 --> 00:00:02.417
No signal generator.

3
00:00:02.858 --> 00:00:06.380
Nothing installed — no driver,
no app, no account.

4
00:00:06.821 --> 00:00:11.521
That's a Raspberry Pi Pico 2,
one USB cable, and a browser tab.

5
00:00:11.962 --> 00:00:14.285
The waveform is coming
from the chip itself.

6
00:00:14.726 --> 00:00:17.720
There's one decision in this
that makes the screen look different

7
00:00:18.161 --> 00:00:20.299
from every other oscilloscope
you've used.

8
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.364
That's what this video is about.

NOTE section

9
00:00:25.406 --> 00:00:28.677
PiLyzer. Three analogue channels,
twelve bits.

10
00:00:29.118 --> 00:00:34.281
An oscilloscope, a spectrum analyser,
an eight-channel logic analyser, a logger.

11
00:00:34.722 --> 00:00:39.471
Two front ends, one instrument:
a native macOS app, and a web page

12
00:00:39.912 --> 00:00:44.461
that talks to the board over WebUSB.
Same protocol, same numbers.

13
00:00:44.903 --> 00:00:47.239
All of it is open,
and the link is below.

NOTE section

14
00:00:50.281 --> 00:00:55.109
Plug the board in. Open the page.
Connect — the browser asks which device,

15
00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.973
and that's the only permission there is.
Run.

16
00:00:58.414 --> 00:01:01.139
A flat line — nothing is driving
that pin yet.

17
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.648
So let's attach something —
without attaching anything.

18
00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:07.261
The firmware has a signal generator in it.

19
00:01:07.702 --> 00:01:11.878
A sine, and white, pink and brown noise,
on four pins of the board.

20
00:01:12.319 --> 00:01:15.793
Wire one of them back to an input
and the instrument measures itself.

21
00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:18.709
One kilohertz.
There it is in the spectrum.

22
00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.024
And that's the whole set-up cost:
a board and a cable.

23
00:01:22.466 --> 00:01:27.038
Chrome on Android is a WebUSB host too,
so this also runs on a phone.

24
00:01:27.479 --> 00:01:29.073
Same page. No app.

NOTE section

25
00:01:32.114 --> 00:01:34.850
Here's the problem
I could not design my way around.

26
00:01:35.292 --> 00:01:38.493
A Pico's converter reads
zero to three point three volts.

27
00:01:38.934 --> 00:01:41.568
That's the whole range.
There's no negative side.

28
00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:45.723
There's no range switch, because there's
nothing in front of it to switch.

29
00:01:46.164 --> 00:01:48.982
A normal oscilloscope shows you
volts at the probe tip.

30
00:01:49.423 --> 00:01:53.065
It knows what's in front of it —
an attenuator, a gain stage —

31
00:01:53.506 --> 00:01:56.957
so it can work backwards and tell you
what you actually connected.

32
00:01:57.398 --> 00:01:59.137
A bare Pico knows none of that.

33
00:01:59.578 --> 00:02:04.039
So when the number on screen says
one point six five volts, what does it mean?

34
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.787
I made it mean the one thing
it can always mean:

35
00:02:07.228 --> 00:02:08.770
this is what the converter saw.

36
00:02:09.211 --> 00:02:13.514
Not what I think you connected. Not a
corrected number. The volts at the pin.

37
00:02:13.955 --> 00:02:17.934
If your circuit sits at mid rail —
and most single-supply circuits do —

38
00:02:18.375 --> 00:02:20.280
the trace sits in the middle of the screen,

39
00:02:20.721 --> 00:02:25.199
and the instrument draws that bias as a
dotted line instead of quietly subtracting it.

40
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.870
You can tell it where the bias is.
It will draw it. It will not take it out.

41
00:02:30.311 --> 00:02:34.217
Because a number that has been silently
adjusted is a number you cannot check.

42
00:02:34.658 --> 00:02:38.856
And on a home-made front end, the thing most
likely to be wrong is the adjustment.

43
00:02:39.297 --> 00:02:43.351
When there's a correction — a known voltage
read one and a half per cent high —

44
00:02:43.792 --> 00:02:46.715
it says so, as a percentage, on the panel.

45
00:02:47.156 --> 00:02:49.931
Corrections you can see
are corrections you can argue with.

46
00:02:50.372 --> 00:02:53.157
That's the decision.
Everything else follows from it.

NOTE section

47
00:02:56.198 --> 00:02:59.326
Back to that generator, because
there's something worth knowing.

48
00:02:59.767 --> 00:03:04.038
The RP2350 has no
digital-to-analogue converter.

49
00:03:04.479 --> 00:03:06.224
So the sine is not a voltage.

50
00:03:06.665 --> 00:03:10.191
It's a pulse-width carrier at five hundred
and eighty-six kilohertz,

51
00:03:10.632 --> 00:03:13.031
whose duty cycle follows a sine table.

52
00:03:13.472 --> 00:03:16.052
Wire that straight to an input
and this is what you get.

53
00:03:16.493 --> 00:03:21.005
Garbage — you're sampling a square wave
at half a megahertz, with a converter at

54
00:03:21.446 --> 00:03:25.193
a few hundred kilohertz,
and everything folds back on itself.

55
00:03:25.634 --> 00:03:30.431
One resistor and one capacitor —
a kilohm and ten nanofarads — and it's a sine.

56
00:03:30.872 --> 00:03:35.030
Three point one five volts peak to peak,
which is what it's designed to be.

57
00:03:35.471 --> 00:03:39.416
That's a real lesson and not a detour:
a digital pin is never a voltage

58
00:03:39.858 --> 00:03:41.480
until something has averaged it.

NOTE section

59
00:03:44.521 --> 00:03:45.861
Numbers, honestly.

60
00:03:46.302 --> 00:03:50.530
One channel: about four hundred and
ninety-five thousand samples a second.

61
00:03:50.972 --> 00:03:55.596
Two: two hundred and forty-seven.
Three: one hundred and sixty-five —

62
00:03:56.037 --> 00:03:58.613
they share one converter,
so they share the rate.

63
00:03:59.054 --> 00:04:03.767
Twelve bits across three point three volts,
which is eight hundred microvolts a count.

64
00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:08.621
The logic analyser is eight channels,
up to a hundred and fifty megasamples,

65
00:04:09.062 --> 00:04:11.102
and it costs eight resistors and a header —

66
00:04:11.543 --> 00:04:14.564
which makes it, per yen,
the best thing on the board.

67
00:04:15.005 --> 00:04:18.084
What it will not do: it will not read
a negative voltage,

68
00:04:18.525 --> 00:04:21.169
it will not read anything
above three point three volts,

69
00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:22.907
and it will survive neither.

70
00:04:23.348 --> 00:04:27.104
On a bare Pico, that's the deal.
There's nothing in front of the pin.

NOTE section

71
00:04:30.146 --> 00:04:31.890
Which brings us to the next one.

72
00:04:32.331 --> 00:04:35.249
Most things you want to measure
swing about zero volts —

73
00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:38.944
audio, a function generator,
anything alternating.

74
00:04:39.385 --> 00:04:41.391
A bare Pico cannot see them at all.

75
00:04:41.832 --> 00:04:46.421
So next time: one op amp, a coupling capacitor
and two resistors,

76
00:04:46.862 --> 00:04:50.724
and a signal that swings about zero
becomes a signal a Pico can read.

77
00:04:51.165 --> 00:04:54.825
We'll also talk about the op amp I tried first
that could not do it,

78
00:04:55.266 --> 00:04:58.095
and the resistor I got wrong
that cost me an afternoon —

79
00:04:58.537 --> 00:05:00.971
and how the instrument itself
is what caught both.

80
00:05:01.412 --> 00:05:06.084
Link to the code below. If there's a Pico 2
in a drawer, this is an afternoon.
